    "done_when": "First reproduce at base: a review whose finding reads FINDING tests/test_x.py:12 blocking: the guard never fires parses to exactly the keys file, line and text (the currency report at /home/ITER/mcintos/.config/reckon/crew/reports/reckon/s22-coord/scoring-currency/report.md shows the call). Then the vocabulary is declared once, as a module-level constant the prompt text and the parser both read, with at most three values, one of them the blocking value; reckon/crew/prompts/review.md tells a reviewer how to mark a finding with it. The parser records a severity only when the finding states one of the declared values, leaves the field absent otherwise, and keeps file, line and text unchanged for every finding shape it parsed before. A stored review record round-trips the field through the production reader. A new tests/test_a_finding_says_whether_it_blocks.py asserts each declared value parses, an undeclared word stays in the text with no severity, a finding with no severity has no severity key, existing finding shapes parse exactly as before, and the prompt names every declared value. The named negative control (the parser ignores severity) reddens the blocking case. The gate is that new test plus tests/test_review_findings_compose_a_repair.py, tests/test_readers_compose_the_record.py and tests/test_review_composition.py, run at base and at head. Each arm is recorded as JSON carrying completed true and a literal command with absolute paths and no angle-bracket placeholder; each log names its revision on the first line and ends with a bare line reading EXIT= followed by the exit code. Every pytest process uses a basetemp private to that process. The head arm adds no failure id; any added id means status blocked, naming each id. ruff check and ruff format --check over the changed paths report no finding the base did not. Scratch goes under /tmp in a directory named for this run and is deleted before status complete, printed and listed in artifacts. An 